`testcase.py` calls `faker.ipv4_public(network=False, address_class="a")`, which goes through `_get_public_networks_and_weights`. That helper starts `public_networks` as the single class-A supernet `[_IPv4Constants._network_classes["a"]]`, i.e. `[IPv4Network('0.0.0.0/1')]`, and passes it into `_exclude_ipv4_networks` together with `_private_networks + _excluded_networks`. Inside `_exclude_ipv4_networks`, `networks_to_exclude` is sorted by prefix length, and on each loop iteration `networks` is reassigned from the flattened `nested_networks`. For this input, only exclusions that overlap the class-A space change `networks`: `10.0.0.0/8`, then `0.0.0.0/8`, then `127.0.0.0/8`, then `100.64.0.0/10`; the later exclusions do not overlap and therefore leave `networks` unchanged, so they do not create new assigned values.

